                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for beauty editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the credential or the test.</strong> "As a cosmetic chemist" or "I wore this SPF daily for 8 weeks in three climates" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the angle the staff missed.</strong> Not "how to layer skincare." Something like "why the 10-step routine breaks down for rosacea-prone skin."</li>
              <li><strong>Bring your own imagery.</strong> Swatches, before-and-after, technique steps. Beauty editors will not run stock for a hands-on piece.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the tier's standard.</strong> Ingredient and skincare pieces need an expert author or reviewer. Technique pieces need a portfolio.</li>
              <li><strong>Send one clip</strong> in a similar style and your bio.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any brand relationship</strong> up front, especially for products you formulate or sell.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had no product to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it is a walkthrough of your line, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a generic "best moisturisers 2026" listicle. Every site has a rolling one, and claims need an expert.</li>
              <li>Send skincare science with no dermatologist or chemist attached.</li>
              <li>Recycle a press release as a "review."</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links, or point exact-match anchors ("best vitamin C serum") at your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated copy with no real testing. In beauty it reads as generic immediately.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on fact-checked sites with a named expert byline will do more for rankings and trust than a long list of thin beauty-blog links. Use this list to find your few.</p></div>
